---
import AuthorCard from "../components/AuthorCard.astro";
import { AUTHOR, SITE } from "../consts";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  name: `About ${SITE.name}`,
  url: new URL("/about/", Astro.site ?? SITE.url).href,
  description: SITE.description,
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About — ${SITE.name}`}
  description={`Who writes ${SITE.name}, who it is for, how each piece is produced, and what we refuse to do.`}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="container">
    <main id="main">
      <div class="page-head">
        <h1>About {SITE.name}</h1>
        <p>
          We help Malaysians understand the world: what is happening, why it is
          happening, and what it means here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:32px;">
        <h2>What this site does</h2>
        <p>
          Most international news reaches Malaysia already summarised, in a form
          built for people who already follow the subject. A tariff decision, a
          central-bank move, a shift in a supply network: the report says what
          happened, rarely why, and almost never what it changes for a household
          in Shah Alam or a small exporter in Johor.
        </p>
        <p>
          {SITE.name} takes the primary documents — the gazette, the statistical
          release, the policy statement — and answers three questions in order:
          what happened, why now, and what it means for Malaysia. The third
          question is the reason the site exists; the first two are how we earn
          the right to ask it.
        </p>

        <h2>Who this is for</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Families watching prices.</strong> What is moving, what is
            not, and which line on the bill is actually connected to the story.
            Start with <a href="/category/malaysia-impact/">Malaysia Impact</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Students and young workers.</strong> Where the jobs are
            moving and what a course choice looks like five years out. Start with{" "}
            <a href="/category/tech-ai/">Tech &amp; AI</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Workers, teachers and small businesses.</strong> Trade rules,
            costs and the economy, written so that nobody needs a second article
            to understand the first. Start with{" "}
            <a href="/category/economy/">Economy &amp; Trade</a> or{" "}
            <a href="/category/world/">World</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Six publics, one standard: a reader who has never opened a foreign news
          section should finish a piece able to say what changed and why it
          matters to them.
        </p>

        {/*
          The id is the anchor the article bylines point at. Google's guidance treats a byline as
          more credible when it resolves to a page explaining who is behind it; without this id the
          link resolved to the top of the page, which made the claim weaker than the markup implied.
        */}
        <h2 id="who">Who is behind this</h2>
        <p>
          {AUTHOR.name} — {AUTHOR.role}. An editorial desk rather than a named
          correspondent, deliberately: the drafting is AI-assisted, and inventing
          a person to make that feel more authoritative would be a fabrication in
          the one place a reader looks to decide whether to trust the page.
        </p>

        <h2>How each piece is produced</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Numbers first, and traceable.</strong> Every figure carries a
            value, a unit, a period, the name of the document and the day it was
            retrieved. A number that cannot carry all five does not go in.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A quote check, not a vibe check.</strong> A figure survives
            only when the sentence it came from can be found verbatim in the
            fetched source. Numbers that fail are dropped, not softened.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The Malaysian consequence, named.</strong> Not "could affect
            the economy" — an industry, a job, a price, a procedure, a group, an
            institution.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Limits stated out loud.</strong> Every piece has a
            <em> How confident</em> section naming what is confirmed, what is
            inferred, and what would change the answer.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>AI, honestly</h2>
        <p>
          Drafting and research are automated; the sources, the arithmetic and the
          structure are checked by machine gates before anything publishes, and
          anything carrying legal risk is held for human review. The disclosure
          record is kept in the repository. We would rather tell you this than
          imply a newsroom we do not have.
        </p>

        <h2>What this site is not</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Not a mouthpiece.</strong> Not affiliated with, funded by, or
            speaking for any government, party, chamber or company.
          </li>
          <li>Not a news aggregator — no rewriting somebody else's story.</li>
          <li>Not legal, tax or investment advice.</li>
          <li>
            No tracking pixels, no sponsored posts dressed up as analysis, no
            advertising network.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          If a figure is wrong it gets corrected, and the change is recorded in a
          dated note on the article itself, under Corrections and updates. The
          note states what changed, not just that something did, and the
          machine-readable date the article was last modified moves only when that
          note is added — never on a schedule. Email{" "}
          <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`}>{AUTHOR.email}</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every piece also carries an internal review date: the point at which its
          central figure is expected to have moved. When one arrives, the piece is
          rechecked and either updated with a note or deliberately left alone.
          Nothing is re-dated to look fresh.
        </p>
      </div>
    </main>

    <aside class="sidebar">
      <AuthorCard />
    </aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
